- **P0 — “Pixel-identical solo vs split” is an unsupported premise, not a requirement.** Solo and split are different modes with different identification needs. A split pane must identify itself; a solo pane is already identified by the selected titlebar chip. Forcing identical chrome makes the common case pay for complexity needed only in the exceptional case.  
  **Fix:** Keep identity chrome conditional. Show pane identity in splits; in solo, expose actions without repeating identity—ideally in the existing terminal tab strip or titlebar.

- **P0 — The plan conflates control ownership with control placement.** Run and Open In acting on the wrong split member is a semantic bug. It does not follow that every pane needs a permanent 28pt header, card, shadow, and tint. The necessary architectural change is target-explicit actions such as `runOrFocusRunCommand(for:)`; the proposed visual container is incidental.  
  **Fix:** Implement target-explicit commands first. Then test three placements independently: split-only pane header, terminal-strip trailing controls, and a compact overlay revealed on pane focus/hover.

- **P0 — It creates two simultaneous workroom navigation systems.** The titlebar chip names and selects a workroom; the proposed pane header repeats the same cube and `project / workroom` label. In solo mode these elements identify the exact same object within roughly 28px of each other. Users will experience this as wasted vertical space and conceptual duplication, not reinforcement. See [WorkroomTabBar.swift](/Users/joelmoss/dev/workroom/macapp/WorkroomApp/Views/WorkroomTabBar.swift:1) and [WorkroomSplitView.swift](/Users/joelmoss/dev/workroom/macapp/WorkroomApp/Views/WorkroomSplitView.swift:264).  
  **Fix:** Pick one identity surface. Either keep titlebar chips and suppress the solo pane label, or remove/reduce titlebar chips once panes self-identify. Do not ship both as permanent chrome.

- **P0 — The card treatment spends the terminal’s visual budget on container decoration.** Every solo workroom would gain four nested signals: outer gutter, inset, rounded card, shadow, and accent wash. The terminal—the primary working surface—then looks embedded in a selected dashboard tile. Persistent accent tint also weakens accent as a signal for activity, focus, selection, links, and command state.  
  **Fix:** Make the terminal surface visually dominant. Use geometry or a one-pixel focus edge for split boundaries. Reserve tint and shadow for drag/drop, transient focus transitions, or overlays—not the permanent solo state.

- **P1 — The proposed toolbar is a likely dumping ground.** “Workroom UI has nowhere to grow” is not a product requirement. It invites future features to accumulate as unexplained glyphs in a narrow pane header. Split panes make the problem worse: every added action is duplicated two to four times while available width shrinks.  
  **Fix:** Define a placement policy before creating the container: frequent stateful actions visible; secondary actions in a workroom menu; terminal actions in the terminal strip; global actions in the window titlebar. Set a compact-width behavior now, including what collapses into an overflow menu.

- **P1 — The plan dismisses growing or restructuring the titlebar without testing it.** The existing titlebar already has adaptive scrolling, pinned controls, and overflow machinery. The assertion that it is “out of room” is contradicted by the plan removing Open In and Run from it, which creates room. A selected-workroom command cluster remains coherent in solo mode.  
  **Fix:** Use a hybrid model: selected-workroom controls remain in the window titlebar for solo mode; split members receive local controls because ambiguity exists only there. If mode transitions cause movement, test whether that cost is actually worse than permanent duplicate chrome.

- **P1 — Dropping titlebar chips was dismissed by decree.** Once every visible pane carries its own identity and controls, chips become a second representation of the same open-workroom set. Their remaining roles—switching, ordering, opening, and split-group indication—could be handled by a smaller switcher or sidebar. Keeping chips while adding pane headers guarantees long-term redundancy.  
  **Fix:** Prototype the reciprocal design: pane headers always identify workrooms, while the titlebar contains a compact workroom switcher rather than persistent chips. Compare task completion and usable terminal height against the proposed design before committing.

- **P1 — “Always present” ignores progressive disclosure.** Run and editor controls are low-frequency for many terminal sessions, and unavailable states already cause them to disappear. A permanent header may frequently contain nothing beyond a repeated name. That is 28pt of dead chrome plus 10pt of horizontal framing.  
  **Fix:** Make the header conditional on information value: always in multi-pane mode; solo only when there are actionable workroom controls, or revealed on hover/focus. Persist an “Always show workroom header” preference only if user demand appears.

- **P1 — The plan optimizes implementation shape as if it validates the UX.** Removing `_ConditionalContent`, deleting the `compact` axis, and standardizing geometry make the code simpler, but those are implementation benefits purchased by permanently changing the product’s common-case layout. “Less branching” is not evidence that users want the result.  
  **Fix:** Preserve the stable terminal host while varying only chrome values or overlays. The code already demonstrates value-level gating; identity safety does not require visually identical solo and split modes.

- **P1 — Narrow split behavior is unspecified.** Two instances of project name, workroom name, Open In, Run/Stop/Restart, divider, and close consume substantial width. At realistic three-pane or small-window widths, the name will collapse while opaque icon groups dominate—the opposite of the header’s identification purpose.  
  **Fix:** Specify width classes. For example: full label and actions above 420pt; shortened label plus primary action at 280–419pt; identity tooltip and overflow menu below 280pt. Add snapshot/UI coverage for minimum supported widths, not merely element existence.

- **P1 — Focus is overloaded with too many signals.** The focused pane would have brighter text, accent cube, accent-tinted card, stronger shadow, and selected accessibility state, while its titlebar chip is also selected. This is excessive and risks making unfocused terminals look disabled.  
  **Fix:** Use one persistent focus signal inside the content—preferably a subtle edge or header foreground change. Keep terminal opacity and colors stable unless reduced contrast is explicitly validated.

- **P2 — Solo loses meaningful space on every edge for no demonstrated gain.** The plan changes a 2/6pt terminal gutter into 6pt outer padding plus 4pt card padding and adds a 28pt header. On a terminal-first app, this affects rows, columns, wrapping, and side-by-side comparisons. The screenshot arithmetic verifies implementation, not value.  
  **Fix:** Establish a terminal viewport budget. Require the proposal to preserve the current solo terminal rows and near-equivalent columns at common window sizes.

- **P2 — The “same controls everywhere” rule creates unstable layouts.** Open In disappears when no editor exists; Run disappears without a command; Stop expands to Stop plus Restart; missing targets remove both. The pane identity label will continually gain and lose horizontal room.  
  **Fix:** Reserve a consistent action region or collapse workroom actions into one menu with an optional primary Run/Stop button. Avoid reflowing identity text when run state changes.

- **P2 — The issue has no success metric.** Verification is almost entirely structural: elements exist, counts match, hover works, geometry measures correctly. None tests whether users identify panes faster, invoke commands more reliably, or resent reduced terminal space.  
  **Fix:** Define success before implementation: zero wrong-target actions, faster split-member identification, no reduction in solo terminal rows at the standard window size, and no duplicated workroom title in the default solo presentation.

The highest-leverage reframe is: **fix target ambiguity in splits without taxing solo mode**. Implement target-explicit command APIs, retain the current solo titlebar controls, and add a restrained split-only pane affordance. Separately prototype either pane-owned identity or titlebar-chip identity; do not institutionalize both.

